# Monte Carlo estimate of pi: how the mean absolute error scales with N

## Method

Points (x, y) are drawn uniformly in [0,1)^2, as interleaved (x, y) pairs from one stream, with `numpy.random.default_rng(2026 + r)`, run index r = 0..199 (seeds 2026..2225), and pi is estimated as 4·#(x²+y² ≤ 1)/N. For each N in 10^3 … 10^7 there are 200 independent estimates, and the error is |estimate − `math.pi`|. The generator is reset per N, so for a given run the points at smaller N are a prefix of those at larger N (common random numbers): runs are independent within each N, but the five per-N means are positively dependent. Points were actually sampled, not drawn from a binomial. Code, raw per-run estimates and metadata are in `experiment-repo` (`code/simulate.py`, `code/analyze.py`, `results/runs.csv`, `results/summary.csv`, `results/fit.json`; revision 0ceece50963ed7752e013704f3e17ecd4d7cf9c2; NumPy 2.4.6).

## Results

| N | runs | mean absolute error | standard error | theory (1.3103/√N) | (obs − theory)/SE |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 10^3 | 200 | 4.1887e-02 | 2.27e-03 | 4.1434e-02 | +0.20 |
| 10^4 | 200 | 1.4406e-02 | 7.51e-04 | 1.3103e-02 | +1.74 |
| 10^5 | 200 | 4.3101e-03 | 2.19e-04 | 4.1434e-03 | +0.76 |
| 10^6 | 200 | 1.3988e-03 | 7.33e-05 | 1.3103e-03 | +1.21 |
| 10^7 | 200 | 4.1946e-04 | 2.23e-05 | 4.1434e-04 | +0.23 |

Standard error is that of the mean over the 200 runs. The theory column is the normal-approximation value √(32·p(1−p)/π)/√N ≈ 1.3103/√N with p = π/4.

## Scaling fit

An unweighted least-squares line of ln(mean absolute error) on ln N through the five points gives

**exponent = −0.5012**, 95% interval **[−0.5162, −0.4861]** (paired bootstrap over runs, B = 50,000 replicates, bootstrap seed 20261018, percentile endpoints; whole run vectors are resampled so the dependence across N is kept), R² = 0.9996.

The expected exponent −1/2 lies inside the interval; the deviation is −0.0012, well under the interval half-width of about 0.015. As a sensitivity check, the conventional regression t interval (3 degrees of freedom, t = 3.182) is [−0.5192, −0.4831], which also contains −0.5; it assumes independent homoscedastic log residuals, which the shared seeds violate, so it is descriptive only. Dropping any one N moves the slope between −0.4953 and −0.5096.

